{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q3817812","formula":"Zn₃(AsO₄)₂·8H₂O","hill":"As2H16O16Zn3","elements":["H","O","Zn","As"],"composition":{"H":16.0,"O":16.0,"As":2.0,"Zn":3.0},"names":{"en":"Köttigite"},"descriptions":{"en":"Köttigite is a rare hydrated zinc arsenate which was discovered in 1849 and named by James Dwight Dana in 1850 in honour of Otto Friedrich Köttig (1824–1892), a German chemist from Schneeberg, Saxony, who made the first chemical analysis of the mineral. It has the formula Zn3(AsO4)2·8H2O and it is a dimorph of metaköttigite, which means that the two minerals have the same formula, but a different structure: köttigite is monoclinic and metaköttigite is triclinic. There are several minerals with similar formulae but with other cations in place of the zinc."},"fame":8,"pubchemCid":null,"mass":null,"charge":0,"smiles":null,"inchikey":null,"iupac":null,"classes":[{"qid":"Q12089225","names":{"en":"mineral species","ru":"действительный вид минералов","uk":"мінеральний вид"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"crystal","source":"materialsproject","ref":"mp-1199333","atoms":96,"license":"CC BY 4.0, Materials Project"},"structure":{"cell":[[10.44,0.0,0.0],[0.0,13.951,0.0],[-1.283,0.0,4.627]],"kind":"crystal","atoms":[{"x":0.0,"y":0.0,"z":0.0,"el":"Zn"},{"x":-1.283,"y":0.0,"z":4.627,"el":"Zn"},{"x":0.0,"y":13.951,"z":0.0,"el":"Zn"},{"x":-1.283,"y":13.951,"z":4.627,"el":"Zn"},{"x":10.44,"y":0.0,"z":0.0,"el":"Zn"},{"x":9.157,"y":0.0,"z":4.627,"el":"Zn"},{"x":10.44,"y":13.951,"z":0.0,"el":"Zn"},{"x":9.157,"y":13.951,"z":4.627,"el":"Zn"},{"x":5.22,"y":12.394,"z":0.0,"el":"Zn"},{"x":3.937,"y":12.394,"z":4.627,"el":"Zn"},{"x":5.22,"y":1.557,"z":0.0,"el":"Zn"},{"x":3.937,"y":1.557,"z":4.627,"el":"Zn"},{"x":6.371,"y":0.0,"z":2.883,"el":"As"},{"x":6.371,"y":13.951,"z":2.883,"el":"As"},{"x":2.787,"y":0.0,"z":1.744,"el":"As"},{"x":2.787,"y":13.951,"z":1.744,"el":"As"},{"x":0.405,"y":12.838,"z":2.935,"el":"H"},{"x":0.405,"y":1.113,"z":2.935,"el":"H"},{"x":8.752,"y":1.113,"z":